  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAfter a day of scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms\nyesterday, the overall threat for precipitation decreases some \nfor Thursday. The shortwave trough that swept across the state \nyesterday is moving into eastern WY, taking most of the \nthunderstorm activity with it. WV imagery shows the main \nmoisture pool shifting east with it, while a push of drier air \nmoves in from the west. The lack of midlevel energy and the \ndecrease in moisture will keep thunderstorm activity much more \nisolated today, mainly along the higher elevations of the \nAbsarokas and Winds. With PW values holding near 1.00 late this \nafternoon, will likely continue the Flash Flood Watch through \nthis evening, but only for those mountain ranges, after \nyesterday's saturating rainfall keeps some watershed prone for \nmore runoff.\n\n\nThe H5 ridge builds across the Rockies for the weekend, centered\nstill on the Four Corners region. Temperatures jump nearly 10\ndegrees from Thursday to Friday, with highs in the upper 80s to\nthe mid 90s. Saturday will see continued warming, with highs\nfrom the mid 90s for western valleys and south WY, to upper 90s\nand low 100s for northern and central WY. Monsoon moisture will\nstill be able to draw into the state, but will be decreased\nenough to limit shower activity to higher elevations. The\nflooding risk will these will also be much lower. This warmer\npattern with more isolated daytime showers on higher elevations\nlook to remain the trend well into the upcoming week.",
